==============================================================================
Consequences for the k = 6 cycle cases (single-centre content only):
  * A palette of size 3 is compatible with a cycle through ANY pair of
    labels, so at k = 6 no palette bound can forbid a cycle of any length.
    The single-centre constraints are powerless at every cyclic centre,
    consistently with the 'powerless class' theorem cited in the brief.
  * For ell = 6 the admissible 2-element palettes are exactly the 15 - 6 = 9
    diagonals of C_6 (6 at distance 2, 3 at distance 3); every singleton is
    excluded.  A 6-cycle at k = 6 therefore leaves a 3-periodic structure:
    distances 2 and 3 along C_6 are the two orbits of the dihedral group.
  * For ell = 5, k = 6: the admissible pairs are the 5 diagonals {i,i+2} and
    the 5 pairs {i,X} with the single extra label X.
  * For ell = 4, k = 6: the admissible pairs are the 2 diagonals, the 8 pairs
    {i,X},{i,Y} with the two extra labels, and {X,Y}.
  * For ell = 3, k = 6: 35 admissible palettes, including P = T (see
    triangle_stratum.py for the full table and its discussion).
==============================================================================
